# roar

`roar` is a CLI for ML/data pipeline provenance. It wraps normal commands with `roar run` to automatically capture inputs, outputs, command args, timing, and git context. Lineage is stored locally in `.roar/roar.db` and can be published to GLaaS.

## Core principle

**You don't change your code to use roar — you change how you launch it.**
Replace `python train.py` with `roar run python train.py`. Everything else (file reads, file writes, package versions, git state) is captured automatically.

## When to use roar

- The user wants reproducibility, lineage, or "what produced this artifact"
- They're running ML training, data processing, or any pipeline where inputs/outputs matter
- They've asked you to investigate provenance, trace artifacts back to producers, or compare runs

## Essential commands

| Command | What it does |
|---|---|
| `roar init` | Set up `.roar/` in the current project (one-time) |
| `roar run <cmd>` | Execute `<cmd>` with full provenance capture |
| `roar build <cmd>` | Track a build/setup step (separate from main pipeline) |
| `roar status` | Show active session summary |
| `roar log` | List jobs in the active session |
| `roar show [REF]` | Inspect a session, job, or artifact. `REF` can be `@1` (step), 8-char hex (job UID), or longer hex (artifact hash) |
| `roar show --all [REF]` | Same, but disable truncation of packages, env vars, and job lists |
| `roar dag` | Visualize the local execution DAG |
| `roar lineage <hash>` | Inspect lineage for a tracked artifact |
| `roar inputs <ref>` | Show root input artifacts for a target |
| `roar scope use <org>/<project>` | Set where this repo publishes |
| `roar register` | Publish the session's lineage to GLaaS (`--dry-run` to preview) |
| `roar reproduce <hash> --run` | Re-execute a lineage and check it rebuilds |
| `roar label set <target> <ref> k=v` | Name/annotate a dag, job, or artifact |

## Common workflows

### Investigating a session
```bash
roar status              # what's the active session?
roar log                 # what jobs ran?
roar show @1             # details of step 1
roar show --all @1       # full details (no truncation)
```

### Tracing an artifact
```bash
roar show <hash>         # who made it, what consumed it
roar lineage <hash>      # full upstream/downstream graph
roar inputs <hash>       # root inputs
```

### Running with provenance
```bash
roar run python train.py --epochs 10
roar run bash scripts/preprocess.sh
roar build pip install -r requirements.txt   # build step, not main pipeline
```

## Sourced vs unsourced inputs

Not every input a run reads will exist on another machine. roar splits inputs into two kinds, and the distinction is what determines whether a lineage actually reproduces:

- **Sourced** — the input has a tracked origin: either a previous job in the lineage produced it, or it's covered by the recorded git commit. Anyone who reproduces the lineage gets it.
- **Unsourced** — nothing tracked produced it *and* it isn't in git. A loose file that pre-existed the run — a manual download, a `/tmp` file, an uncommitted script. It won't exist when the lineage is reproduced elsewhere. This is the single most common reason a complete-looking lineage doesn't reproduce.

roar warns at run time when a job reads an unsourced input, and you can list them:

```bash
roar inputs --unsourced <ref>    # unsourced inputs for a job or artifact
```

To make an unsourced input sourced:

- **Code, configs, scripts** — commit them to git. (A program you launch directly, e.g. `roar run ./script.sh`, is itself an input; an uncommitted one is unsourced.)
- **External data** — ingest it through roar so the retrieval is recorded: `roar get <url>` or `roar run wget <url>`. The resulting Get job becomes the input's tracked producer.

`roar register` and `roar reproduce` print a reproducibility checklist whose "all inputs sourced" line reflects exactly this. When the user wants reproducibility, treat unsourced inputs as something to resolve, not ignore.

## The reproducibility checklist

`roar register` (and `roar put`) print this punchlist as their receipt; `roar reproduce` prints the subset that bears on whether a reproduction can run. It is the operational definition of "reproducible" — when a user asks for a reproducible pipeline, these are the checks to drive to green:

| Check | Passes when |
|---|---|
| code committed to git | the run happened inside a git repo with the code versioned |
| single git commit across all steps | the session didn't span multiple commits (see gotcha below) |
| commit reachable on a remote | a shareable remote exists — `git remote add origin <url>` |
| all inputs sourced | no unsourced inputs (see above) |
| all artifact paths in tracked directories | output dirs are known to git; add a `.gitkeep` to empty ones. Diagnose with `roar status --untracked-dirs` |
| runtime captured | interpreter + packages recorded — automatic under `roar run` |
| lineage saved on glaas.ai | `roar register` has published it |

The last two rows appear only on register/put receipts, not on `roar reproduce`.

## Publishing to GLaaS

Capture is local; publishing is a separate, deliberate step.

```bash
roar scope list                      # what scopes can I publish to?
roar scope use <org>/<project>       # set this repo's default publication scope
roar scope status                    # what's active now?
roar register --dry-run              # preview: shows the checklist, publishes nothing
roar register                        # publish the session's lineage
roar reproduce <hash> --run          # re-execute and verify it rebuilds
roar reproduce <hash> --lineage --run  # reproduce a whole lineage, not one artifact
```

- **Register the session, not just a file.** `roar register` with no target publishes the active session's lineage; passing an artifact narrows it.
- **`--dry-run` first.** It renders the same checklist and runs secret detection without publishing, so it's the cheap way to see what you're about to expose. Registration prompts before proceeding if secrets are detected; `-y` skips prompts (including that one), so don't reach for `-y` until a dry run is clean.
- **Attribution is decided at register time.** Effective visibility comes from `--public`/`--private` if given, otherwise from the repo's scope. Publishing under an org scope attributes the lineage to that org; a personal scope leaves supplier/author blank.
- **`--anonymous` is not reversible.** It forces public+anonymous and discards attribution *even when auth is configured*. If the lineage is meant to be credited to an org, registering anonymously means re-running the pipeline to fix it — there's no re-attribute. Don't pass it unless anonymity is the actual goal.

## Naming what you publish

Published lineage is read by people. Unnamed, a DAG reads as raw commands and filenames — `python3 train.py` producing `model.pkl` — which is what a reader sees on a public lineage page.

```bash
roar run -n "train model" -- python train.py     # name a step at run time
roar label set job @2 name="train model"         # or rename it afterwards
roar label set dag <session-hash> name="nightly retrain"
roar label set artifact model.pkl name="my-model" \
    description="..." license.id=MIT documentation.url="https://..."
```

Artifact labels also populate the CycloneDX AI-BOM that GLaaS generates for a registered session (`description`, `license.id`, `documentation.url` map to component fields), so naming the model artifact improves both readability and BOM completeness. Label targets are `dag`, `job`, `composite`, and `artifact`.

## Compliance tags

`roar tag` manages *hereditary* tags — compliance facts that travel downstream through the lineage graph, so a model trained on PII-bearing data inherits that fact without anyone re-asserting it. Tags live in the `tag.*` label namespace and are append-only: `roar tag rm` records a removal, it doesn't erase history.

Canonical kinds: `license`, `contains_pii`, `jurisdiction`, `classification`, `special_category`. Targets are `@N` (job step N in the active session) or an artifact hash prefix / path.

```bash
roar tag add license=GPL-3.0 @1     # assert a tag on a step
roar tag show @1                    # current tags
roar tag why contains_pii model.pkl # walk the inheritance path to the human act
roar tag history @1                 # every version of the tag document
```

**Scope, and why `bind` exists.** Tags propagate automatically *within* a session. Crossing a session boundary requires an explicit **bind**: `roar tag bind <artifact>` promotes that artifact's current tags to cross-session scope, so other sessions consuming the same artifact inherit them. `roar tag add` on a named artifact is "born bound" (it writes an implicit bind), while `roar run --add-tag` stays session-scoped until bound. This is deliberate — automatic cross-session propagation on content hash alone would let an incidental collision (two empty files share a hash) contaminate unrelated lineage. `roar tag unbind` revokes. `roar register` takes `--bind`/`--no-bind` to control this at publish time.

Use `roar tag why` when a tag appears somewhere unexpected — it explains the inheritance path rather than leaving you to guess.

## Detection: is a script running under roar?

If a Python script needs to *require* it's being run under `roar run`, add at the top:

```python
from roar import require   # exits with helpful message if not under `roar run`
```

The import itself walks the process tree — no env var spoofing. Set `ROAR_GUARD=0` to bypass in tests.

## Tracer backends

roar captures I/O via one of three tracers (auto-selected, configurable via `roar tracer`):
- **eBPF** — kernel-level, lowest overhead, requires Linux + privileges
- **preload** — `LD_PRELOAD` / `DYLD_INSERT_LIBRARIES`, works on Linux + macOS, no privileges
- **ptrace** — fallback, works anywhere, slower

Workloads should NOT need to know which backend is active.

## Gotchas

- **Clean git state is required.** `roar run` refuses to execute if the git working tree has uncommitted changes — including untracked files. There's no `--dirty` bypass; commit (or stash, or gitignore) first. Lineage ties to a commit, so this is by design.
- **One commit for the whole pipeline — don't commit between steps.** This is the trap the rule above walks you into: if you edit a script between steps, the tree is dirty, so you commit again, and now the session spans multiple commits. `roar reproduce` checks out the *last* one, so the earlier steps replay against code they never ran with — results may differ from the original. The checklist reports this as `single git commit across all steps`, and it fails silently in the sense that every individual step succeeded. Iterate freely while developing; then, for the run you intend to publish, commit once and execute every step start-to-finish without touching a tracked file.
- **Add `.roar/` to `.gitignore` before your first run.** Otherwise it becomes an untracked change that trips the clean-tree check above on the next run. `roar init` prompts to add it; say yes. If you run `roar init -n` (skip) you'll need to add it yourself.
- **Default config filters `/tmp` intermediates.** `ignore_tmp_files = true` in the generated `config.toml` drops files the run *wrote* under `/tmp` (endogenous intermediates). A file the run only *read* from `/tmp` pre-existed it and is kept — but flagged unsourced (see above), since `/tmp` is ephemeral and won't survive. If a run shows missing outputs and you're working under `/tmp`, that's why — move the workdir out of `/tmp` (don't park intermediates there) or edit `.roar/config.toml` to flip the filter. Other default filters hide system reads (`/sys`, `/etc`, `/usr`) and installed-package reads.
- **Ray workers**: `ROAR_WRAP=1` is set on the driver but NOT on Ray workers. `ROAR_SESSION_ID` and `ROAR_JOB_ID` are propagated. Use those if a worker needs to detect provenance context.
- **Osmo workers**: no env var propagation today.
- **Don't make user code import roar** for tracking purposes — capture is transparent. The only exception is `from roar import require` for the abort-if-not-tracked guard.
- **Don't commit `.roar/`** — it's local lineage state. `roar init` offers to add it to `.gitignore`.

## Reading `roar show` output

By default, long lists are truncated:
- Packages: first 15 shown, then `... and N more`
- Env var values: cut at 60 chars
- Producer/consumer jobs: first 5 shown
- Commands: cut at ~50 chars

Use `--all` when investigating to see everything.

## When in doubt

Run `roar --help` or `roar <subcommand> --help`. The CLI is the source of truth — this skill captures only the commonly needed patterns.
